<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torontál County</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Torontál County was a county of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mures-river">Maros</a>, with its seat at Nagybecskerek, now Zrenjanin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Re-established in 1779 when the civil Banat was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, it had a mixed population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and Hungarians. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was divided between the Kingdom of the Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and Hungary, which kept a small part.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torontal-county.jpg" alt="Torontál County" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toront%C3%A1l_v%C3%A1rmegye_Magyarorsz%C3%A1gon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>From 1779 until 1920 the county bordered the Hungarian counties of Bács-Bodrog, Csongrád, Csanád, Arad and Temes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> formed its western border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mures-river">Maros</a> (Mureș) its northern one, and the rivers Aranca, Bega, Timiș and Bârzava flowed through it. The Banat Military Frontier lay along its southern edge until it was abolished in 1873; after that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was its southern border, shared with the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Around 1910 it covered 10,042 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A medieval Torontál county existed before the 15th century, with its seat at Aracs, now part of Novi Bečej, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> took the area in 1552 and placed it in the Temeşvar Eyalet. In 1594 local Christians, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> uprising. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy captured the region in the war of 1716 to 1718, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> it became part of the Banat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeswar</a>. The restored county of 1779 had very different borders from the medieval one, and after a great fire in Nagybecskerek its seat moved to Nagyszentmiklós from 1807 to 1820.</p><p>In 1848 and 1849 the self-proclaimed Serbian Vojvodina claimed the area, and from 1849 to 1860 it belonged to the Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Temes Banat, an Austrian crownland. The county was recreated in January 1861 and enlarged in 1876 with the District of Velika Kikinda, having earlier taken in parts of the former Military Frontier, including Pancsova. In late 1918 the Banat Republic claimed it, and on 25 November an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a> proclaimed the region's union with the Kingdom of Serbia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> of 1920 most of the county went to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>. The north-eastern part went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and lies in Timiș county, while Kiszombor and eight nearby villages south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and are now in Csongrád County.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torontál County” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1778 Maria Theresa returned the civil Banat to Hungary as the counties of Temes, <b>Torontál</b> and Krassó, while its southern strip stayed in the Military Frontier.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-temeswar">Banat of Temeswar</a></li></ul></section>
